<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pati Unus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pati Unus was the second sultan of Demak on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, ruling from about 1518 to 1521. Before taking the throne he led a large Javanese fleet against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> in 1513, which was defeated. He died during a second expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1521 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Trenggana</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pati Unus, also called Yat Sun and known as Pangeran Sabrang Lor, is given the real name Raden Abdul Qadir and is described as the crown prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-patah">Raden Patah</a>, the founder of Demak. The name Sabrang Lor means roughly one who crossed to the north, after his voyages across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> to fight the Portuguese.</p><p>The Portuguese writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a>, in his Suma Oriental, mentions a Pate Unus who was 25 years old in 1513 and calls him the brother-in-law of Pate Rodim, the ruler of Demak, a figure identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-patah">Raden Patah</a>. The accounts of his family therefore do not agree, and his place in the line of Demak's kings sits uneasily with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Trenggana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudera-pasai-sultanate">Samudra Pasai</a> to the Portuguese in 1512 made his task as commander of the Islamic Fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> more urgent, and at the end of that year a fleet set out against the Portuguese fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. It arrived in January 1513 with about 100 ships and 5,000 soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, among them some 30 large junks of 350 to 600 tons carrying artillery made in Java.</p><p>Fernão Pires de Andrade, captain of the fleet that drove it off, wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a> on 22 February 1513 that Pati Unus's own junk had three layers of metal that even the heaviest Portuguese shot could not pierce below the waterline. Pati Unus sailed home and buried his warship as a monument to the struggle, and the expedition is said to have won him the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For the second attack 375 ships were built. Pati Unus sailed with three of his sons, and the fleet left Demak with the blessing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>. As his ship was lowering a boat to land, it was struck by a cannonball from the fortress, and he was killed. Raden Hidayat led the retreat, and Fadhlullah Khan was afterwards made commander of the fleet.</p><p>His first and third sons also died, while the second, Raden Abdullah, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Malays from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> who sailed back with the fleet settled there, and their descendants later helped Raden Abdullah's line spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the land of Pasundan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pati Unus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pati Unus</b> led fleets against Portuguese Malacca in 1513.</blockquote><a href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jepara, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-6.53,110.67&amp;year=1511" aria-label="Jepara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pati Unus"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4211 5557h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4211 5557h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-6.53,110.67&amp;year=1511">What was here in 1511? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
